⟦P0⟧Tutorial: Find and fix vulnerable dependencies with govulncheck⟦P1⟧Govulncheck is a low-noise tool that helps you find and fix vulnerable⟦P2⟧dependencies in your Go projects. It does this by scanning your project's⟦P3⟧dependencies for known vulnerabilities and then identifying any direct or⟦P4⟧indirect calls to those vulnerabilities in your code.⟦P5⟧In this tutorial, you will learn how to use govulncheck to scan a simple⟦P6⟧program for vulnerabilities. You will also learn how to prioritize and⟦P7⟧evaluate vulnerabilities so that you can focus on fixing the most important⟦P8⟧ones first.⟦P9⟧To learn more about govulncheck, see the⟦P10⟧⟦P11⟧govulncheck documentation⟦P12⟧,⟦P13⟧and this ⟦P14⟧blog post on vulnerability management⟦P15⟧ for Go.⟦P16⟧We'd also love to ⟦P17⟧hear your feedback⟦P18⟧.⟦P19⟧Prerequisites⟦P20⟧⟦P21⟧Go.⟦P22⟧ We recommend using the latest version of Go to follow this tutorial.⟦P23⟧For installation instructions, see ⟦P24⟧Installing Go⟦P25⟧.⟦P26⟧⟦P27⟧A code editor.⟦P28⟧ Any editor you have will work fine.⟦P29⟧⟦P30⟧A command terminal.⟦P31⟧ Go works well using any terminal on Linux and Mac, and on ⟦P32⟧ or cmd in Windows.⟦P33⟧The tutorial will take you through the following steps:⟦P34⟧Create a sample Go module with a vulnerable dependency⟦P35⟧Install and run govulncheck⟦P36⟧Evaluate vulnerabilities⟦P37⟧Upgrade vulnerable dependencies⟦P38⟧Create a sample Go module with a vulnerable dependency⟦P39⟧⟦P40⟧Step 1.⟦P41⟧ To begin, create a new folder called ⟦P42⟧ and initialize a Go module.⟦P43⟧(If you are new to Go modules, check out ⟦P44⟧⟦P45⟧⟦P46⟧.⟦P47⟧For example, from your home directory, run the following:⟦P48⟧⟦P49⟧Step 2.⟦P50⟧ Create a file called ⟦P51⟧ within the ⟦P52⟧ folder, and copy⟦P53⟧the following code into it:⟦P54⟧This sample program takes a list of language tags as command line arguments⟦P55⟧and prints a message for each tag indicating if it was parsed successfully,⟦P56⟧the tag is undefined, or whether there was an error while parsing the tag.⟦P57⟧⟦P58⟧Step 3.⟦P59⟧ Run ⟦P60⟧, which will populate the ⟦P61⟧ file with all the⟦P62⟧dependencies required by the code you added to ⟦P63⟧ in the previous step.⟦P64⟧From the ⟦P65⟧ folder, run:⟦P66⟧You should see this output:⟦P67⟧⟦P68⟧Step 4.⟦P69⟧ Open your ⟦P70⟧ file to verify that it looks like this:⟦P71⟧⟦P72⟧Step 5.⟦P73⟧ Downgrade the version of ⟦P74⟧ to ⟦P75⟧, which contains known⟦P76⟧vulnerabilities. Run:⟦P77⟧You should see this output:⟦P78⟧The ⟦P79⟧ file should now read:⟦P80⟧Now, let’s see govulncheck in action.⟦P81⟧Install and run govulncheck⟦P82⟧⟦P83⟧Step 6.⟦P84⟧ Install govulncheck with the ⟦P85⟧ command:⟦P86⟧⟦P87⟧Step 7.⟦P88⟧ From the folder you want to analyze (in this case, ⟦P89⟧). Run:⟦P90⟧You should see this output:⟦P91⟧Interpreting the output⟦P92⟧⟦P93⟧  ⟦P94⟧Note: If you are not using the latest version of Go,⟦P95⟧you may see additional vulnerabilities from the standard library. ⟦P96⟧⟦P97⟧Our code is affected by one vulnerability,⟦P98⟧⟦P99⟧⟦P100⟧⟦P101⟧, because it directly calls⟦P102⟧the ⟦P103⟧ function of ⟦P104⟧ at a vulnerable version⟦P105⟧(⟦P106⟧).⟦P107⟧Another vulnerability, ⟦P108⟧⟦P109⟧⟦P110⟧,⟦P111⟧exists in the ⟦P112⟧ module at ⟦P113⟧.  However, it is reported as⟦P114⟧"Informational" because our code never (directly or indirectly) calls any of⟦P115⟧its vulnerable functions.⟦P116⟧Now, let's evaluate the vulnerabilities and determine an action to take.⟦P117⟧Evaluate vulnerabilities⟦P118⟧a. Evaluate vulnerabilities.⟦P119⟧First, read the description of the vulnerability and determine if it actually⟦P120⟧applies to your code and your use case. If you need more information, visit⟦P121⟧the "More info" link.⟦P122⟧Based on the description, vulnerability ⟦P123⟧ can cause a panic when⟦P124⟧⟦P125⟧ is used to process untrusted user inputs. Let's suppose that we intend⟦P126⟧our program to withstand untrusted inputs, and we are concerned about denial of⟦P127⟧service, so the vulnerability likely applies.⟦P128⟧⟦P129⟧ likely does not affect our code, because our code does not call⟦P130⟧any vulnerable functions from that report.⟦P131⟧b. Decide on an action.⟦P132⟧To mitigate ⟦P133⟧, we have a few options:⟦P134⟧⟦P135⟧Option 1: Upgrade to a fixed version.⟦P136⟧ If there is a fix available,⟦P137⟧we can remove a vulnerable dependency by upgrading to a fixed version of the module.⟦P138⟧⟦P139⟧Option 2: Stop using the vulnerable symbol(s).⟦P140⟧ We could choose to⟦P141⟧remove all calls to the vulnerable function in our code.⟦P142⟧We would need to find an alternative or implement it ourselves.⟦P143⟧In this case, a fix is available, and the ⟦P144⟧ function is integral to our⟦P145⟧program. Let's upgrade our dependency to the "fixed in" version, ⟦P146⟧.⟦P147⟧We decided to deprioritize fixing the informational vulnerability,⟦P148⟧⟦P149⟧, but because it is in the same module as ⟦P150⟧, and because the fixed in version for it is ⟦P151⟧, we can⟦P152⟧easily remove both at the same time by upgrading to ⟦P153⟧.⟦P154⟧Upgrade vulnerable dependencies⟦P155⟧Luckily, upgrading vulnerable dependencies is quite simple.⟦P156⟧⟦P157⟧Step 8.⟦P158⟧ Upgrade ⟦P159⟧ to ⟦P160⟧:⟦P161⟧You should see this output:⟦P162⟧(Note that we could have also chosen to upgrade to ⟦P163⟧, or any other version after ⟦P164⟧).⟦P165⟧⟦P166⟧Step 9.⟦P167⟧ Now run govulncheck again:⟦P168⟧You will now see this output:⟦P169⟧Finally, govulncheck confirms that there are no vulnerabilities found.⟦P170⟧By regularly scanning your dependencies with command govulncheck, you can⟦P171⟧safeguard your codebase by identifying, prioritizing, and addressing⟦P172⟧vulnerabilities.⟦P173⟧
